Z||000011||grinthock||05-15-2001||02:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by p4n1c:<BR><B>Yeah, it would be cool to make my dash powered by Linux.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Actually I really like the way the Olds dashes look.  Usually well laid out except for some of the economy models.<P>As a side note, don't turn the headlights off at night, the dash goes to automatic intense and blinds you.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Now that WOULD be cool, I have the electronics part here alreadyu, it conencts to the ALDL connector and spits out a Serial Data stream at 4800 Baud of all sensor readings including rpm, speed, O2 stuff like that, now all we need is a small 9" LCD or smaller (EASY to get but expensive) and someone to do the programming (of which i could PROBABLY find||||reg||1
Z||000012||Taijiguy||05-15-2001||02:20 PM||||Actually, that's the kind of thing I was looking for. I was thinking of basing this on a basic stamp, with an active LCD display. It could be programmed to scan all the parameters either monitored by the ECM, or output by the ALDL connector. It could be designed to scan and display one at a time, so you wouldn't have to have a hundred different display segments. It could incorporate the above mentiond digitizer to recall, or directly access a particular reading. Hmmmmm.....(thoughtfully scratching my chin with an inquisitive look...)||||reg||14
Z||000013||p4n1c||05-15-2001||02:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B> Now that WOULD be cool, I have the electronics part here alreadyu, it conencts to the ALDL connector and spits out a Serial Data stream at 4800 Baud of all sensor readings including rpm, speed, O2 stuff like that, now all we need is a small 9" LCD or smaller (EASY to get but expensive) and someone to do the programming (of which i could PROBABLY find</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually I'm thinking of doing the same thing with my Palm III that I have.  I'm going to buy another cradle and pull it apart and use the connector, so I can just plug the Palm into the dash.  <P>I also want to have a serial connector for it once I get a laptop, I can use it.  Modify the glove box so the laptop can sit in it and plug in.<BR><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4 OD auto<BR>"Ride the geriatric mobile.. err..  rocket"||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||05-15-2001||02:45 PM||||Im not  an electrical engineer, but I play one on.... no wait, I am an electrical engineer. <P>  If a serial data stream is available from the ECM with all the desired dashboard info in it, the 'easiest' way to go:<P>  Get a 486 IBM thinkpad from ebay with a 9" color active matrix color LCD. It should be fairly easy to get the serial data stream converted to a voltage the serial port on the notebook would accept.<P>  then you just need to write some simple visual basic or C programs to display the data on the LCD in the format you want. Once you get it working on the notebook, strip the notebook apart, hid the motherboard and harddrive behind the dash somewhere, and use the already backlite LCD in place of your instrument panel.<P>  you can get a 486 thinkpad with active matrix LCD for less than $200 bucks on ebay. This would be the easiest way to go.<BR> <BR>   You could get the data stream into a PIC chip or one of those basic stamp kits easy enough, but driving a 640 x 480 color LCD from one is not a simple thing to do, and then you still need to find the LCD somewhere, and the backlight, and the 90vac backlight driver circuit, and brightness adjustments...<P>   a thinkpad has almost everything you need to make a digital dashboard, and it will let you get it all working and tested before you commit to pulling your old dashboard apart.<P>  be careful - this is how people end up starting their own businesses  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||Formula88||05-15-2001||04:50 PM||||Along those lines, is there already a setup I can get for my laptop to plug into the ALDL and read out all of my ECM parameters?  I'm thinking of just in a diag mode now, but incorporating a permanent display sounds pretty cool, too.  But a real-time display of all engine parameters, stored codes, etc. would be great.||||reg||1
