Z||000029||LZeitgeist||05-17-2001||08:19 AM||||cadaver - <P>Yes, there IS a fluorescent red reflective aspect to the V6 gauge package. There is also a screenprinted layer of red *inside* the bezels that would need to be neutralized.<P>Trust me on this. I did extensive tests with the V6 gauges and various colors of fluorescent vinyls, etc. to be able to change the color of the gauges to purple. It's not going to happen unless you completely replace the stock instrument bezels with something without the red coloring in it. Otherwise, it turns brownish and very dark.<P>My most logical thought was, since there is a layer of red in the gauges, to change the fluorescent red "reflector plate" color to fluorescent blue... however, there *is* no such thing as true fluorescent blue, since fluorescent colors are designed to react to ultraviolet light, which is in the blue spectrum. Fluorescent colors also do a very good job of tinting white light reflected off of the color, and this is the aspect that the Fiero gauge package takes advantage of. But, if you see a vinyl or paint color that claims to be 'fluorescent blue', it is actually not fluorescent, but just a very intense blue that will not react to white light the same way that true fluorescent colors do.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000030||Taijiguy||05-17-2001||08:25 AM||||Anybody got a broken, junked, or cheap backlit dash set they can donate or sell cheap for research purposes on this?||||reg||1
